@@ -62,6 +62,41 @@ Canonical Correlation
    :toctree: generated/
 
    CanCorr
+   select_cancorr_penalty
+
+Ridge canonical correlation
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+``CanCorr(endog, exog, penalty=(alpha_x, alpha_y))`` adds trace-relative
+ridge penalties to the sample covariance matrices of ``exog`` and ``endog``.
+Positive penalties permit wide or collinear views. The default penalties are
+zero and preserve the classical calculation, coefficient normalization and
+F-test output. ``standardize=True`` uses training standard deviations;
+``transform(exog, endog)`` applies the fitted preprocessing to new paired rows.
+
+``penalized_roots`` contains the singular values of the ridge objective.
+``cancorr`` contains the Pearson correlations of the fitted training scores;
+the two generally differ under regularization. Components are ordered by
+``penalized_roots``. Held-out score correlations can be negative and their
+signs must not be changed based on validation or test data.
+
+``select_cancorr_penalty`` chooses a penalty pair using the mean signed
+first-component held-out correlation over shuffled K-fold splits. All
+preprocessing is fitted within each training fold, and the selected model is
+refitted on the supplied training data. For example::
+
+    from statsmodels.multivariate.cancorr import select_cancorr_penalty
+
+    grid = [(0.01, 0.01), (0.1, 0.1), (1.0, 1.0)]
+    result = select_cancorr_penalty(y_train, x_train, grid, cv=5,
+                                   standardize=True, rng=123)
+    x_scores, y_scores = result.model.transform(x_test, y_test)
+
+The cross-validation score is a selection criterion. Evaluate generalization
+on separate test observations. Neither numerical agreement with a reference
+implementation nor a large training correlation establishes statistical
+significance. The classical ``corr_test`` F approximation is unavailable for
+penalized fits; a permutation test for these fits is not implemented here.
 
 
