@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# Independent R ridge CCA reference
+
+These small synthetic matrices were generated by `research/run_stage1.py` with
+NumPy's `default_rng(20261104)`. Their hashes and penalties are recorded in
+`reports/stage1/numerical_checks.json`. Each CSV has no header, full float64
+precision and one observation per row, except coefficient/result arrays.
+
+`r_results.csv` and `r_x_weights.csv` / `r_y_weights.csv` are generated by
+**R CCA 1.2.2::rcc**, using these exact input matrices. Python tests compare
+roots, Pearson score correlations and coefficient subspaces against these
+saved R outputs. R is needed only to regenerate the reference, not to run
+the Python tests. In the surrounding HDDA research project the regeneration
+script is `research/r_reference.R`; session information records R 4.6.2 and
+CCA 1.2.2. To regenerate a case independently from these committed inputs:
+
+```r
+library(CCA) # version 1.2.2
+case <- "ridge_lowdim" # alternatively classical or ridge_wide
+x <- as.matrix(read.csv(file.path(case, "x.csv"), header = FALSE))
+y <- as.matrix(read.csv(file.path(case, "y.csv"), header = FALSE))
+alpha <- if (case == "classical") c(0, 0) else c(0.3, 1.2)
+fit <- rcc(x, y, lambda1 = alpha[1] * mean(diag(cov(x))),
+           lambda2 = alpha[2] * mean(diag(cov(y))))
+fit$cor[1:3] # r_results.csv: root
+diag(cor(fit$scores$xscores[, 1:3], fit$scores$yscores[, 1:3]))
+fit$xcoef[, 1:3] # r_x_weights.csv; ycoef analogously
+```
+
+The numerical tests compare coefficient subspaces rather than coefficient
+entries, since signs and score normalizations differ between implementations.
+
+These are assistant-selected numerical unit checks, not the proposal's main
+synthetic benchmark or a generator with prespecified population correlations.
+Inputs are raw, unstandardized. `classical`: alpha=(0,0), n=72,p=6,q=4;
+`ridge_lowdim`: alpha=(0.3,1.2), n=72,p=6,q=4;
+`ridge_wide`: alpha=(0.3,1.2), n=24,p=40,q=31. Compare the first three components.
